{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "The Portuguese Episcopal Conference announced on Jan. 21 that public Masses would be suspended in Portugal from Jan. 23."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "The bishops cited the 'extreme gravity' of the coronavirus crisis as the reason for the suspension."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "They stated that it is a moral imperative for all citizens, particularly Christians, to take maximum sanitary precautions."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Portugal recorded 609,000 COVID-19 cases and 9,920 deaths as of Jan. 22, reflecting a surge in deaths."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "This is the second suspension of public worship by the bishops; the first occurred in March 2020 and was eased at the end of May."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "The bishops announced that public Masses, catechesis, and other pastoral activities requiring gatherings would be suspended from Jan. 23."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "They called on priests to offer livestreamed Masses."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "Earlier in January, the bishops had called for the suspension or postponement of baptisms, confirmations, and marriages."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "They said that funerals could be celebrated according to norms issued in May 2020, requiring social distancing."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "A Lenten retreat for the bishops scheduled for Feb. 22-26 has been canceled."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "The Shrine of Our Lady of Fatima announced it will broadcast three Masses and two rosary prayers daily while public Masses are suspended."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Shrine rector Fr. Carlos Cabecinhas said that following via television is not the same as physical presence but is the only possible way to participate now."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "Human Rights Watch survey finds lives of LGBTQ+ Afghans have dramatically worsened under Taliban rule."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "The report recorded nearly 60 cases of targeted violence against LGBTQ+ people since August 2021."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Heather Barr said that before the Taliban takeover, people had hope for gradual improvement, but on 15 August 2021, all of that ended."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Homosexuality was banned under former president Ashraf Ghani, but the Taliban vowed to take a hard line against LGBT rights, citing sharia law."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "J Lester Feder reported that LGBT Afghans have survived gang-rape, mob attacks, or been hunted by family members who joined the Taliban."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Zeba Gul described that her life worsened after the Taliban came to power, including family abuse before the takeover."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "After the Taliban took power, Zeba Gul's family threw her out, and she was attacked, raped, and held and beaten by the Taliban for three days."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "Faraydoon Fakoori stated that the situation for gender minorities in Afghanistan has worsened after the Taliban's arrival."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Zeba Gul said that the return of the Taliban empowered vigilantes and homophobes, with no fear of punishment."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "A Taliban spokesperson told Bild newspaper that for homosexuals, the only punishments are stoning or standing behind a wall that will fall down on them."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "Clashes broke out Sunday morning in three cities across Israel as police attempted to enforce coronavirus lockdown restrictions in ultra-Orthodox communities."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "In the Jerusalem neighborhood of Mea Shearim, hundreds of people clashed with police attempting to shut down a yeshiva that had opened illegally."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Police officers at the yeshiva were surrounded by a crowd shouting insults and throwing trash and debris, leading to one arrest and the use of water cannons to disperse the crowd."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "In Ashdod, police were confronted while closing an ultra-Orthodox elementary school that had opened in violation of Health Ministry and Education Ministry rules."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Police said several dozen people tried to force their way into the school compound, injured four officers, damaged the fence, and four people were arrested."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "In Bnei Brak, several clashes broke out as police tried to shut down schools linked to the Vizhnitz Hasidic dynasty, whose head rabbi initially ruled that schools should open before backtracking hours later."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "Police stated they would continue to enforce virus restrictions in all communities."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "The lockdown, in its third week and extended till January 31, shut all nonessential businesses and the entire education system except special education."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Last week, clashes broke out in Bnei Brak between police and rioters opposed to lockdown restrictions, with at least six arrests, and the rioters belonged to an extremist faction of the Vizhnitz Hasidic sect."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu condemned the Friday attack in Bnei Brak, stating that 'we will act with a heavy hand against lawbreakers.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Critics accused Netanyahu of avoiding a crackdown on the Haredi public to not anger his ultra-Orthodox coalition partners."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Additional lockdown violations among the ultra-Orthodox emerged Saturday, including a mass gathering in the Sanz Hasidic sect in Netanya."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "Health Minister Yuli Edelstein called for jailing those who broke lockdown rules."
    },
    {
      "id": "13",
      "subevent": "Netanyahu and Edelstein appealed to Rabbi Chaim Kanievsky to influence the community; Kanievsky gave permission for some schools to reopen only if it does not spark confrontation with police."
    },
    {
      "id": "14",
      "subevent": "Haredi communities have suffered disproportionately from the coronavirus due to high infection rates partly caused by rule-breaking."
    },
    {
      "id": "15",
      "subevent": "The Health Ministry reported 2,394 new COVID-19 cases on Saturday, with total infections at 595,097, deaths at 4,361, and 1,228 patients in serious condition."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "A South West Aviation Let L-410, registration UR-TWO, with 20 passengers and 3 crew, impacted Yirol Lake near Yirol City while on approach."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "Local fishermen recovered 19 bodies from the lake; 4 survivors were taken to a hospital with serious injuries."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "On September 10, 2018, authorities reported that 20 people died and 3 passengers survived the accident."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "The Information Minister in Eastern Lakes State reported the aircraft carried 23 people, with 19 bodies recovered and 4 survivors taken to hospital."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "South Sudan's Civil Aviation Authority opened an investigation into the crash."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "On September 9, 2018, The Aviation Herald received a passenger manifest listing South West Aviation as the operator and UR-TWO as the aircraft."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "Russian databases updated information on the aircraft, showing it was previously operated by Universal-Avia and later owned by SlavAir, with tail number UR-TWO."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "The aircraft was restored to flying condition in Rivne, Ukraine in 2016, with the operator name 'South West' and a South Sudanese phone number on the paint scheme."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Ukraine's Aircraft Registry assigned tail number UR-TWO to the L-410 on April 11, 2018, with owner SlavAir Company and no operator mentioned."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "On November 19, 2018, Ukraine's NBAAI reported that SlavAir's L-410 UR-TWO crashed at Yirol, killing 20 and injuring 3, with SSCAA investigating."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Between October 29 and November 2, 2018, the NBAAI read out and decrypted parametric data from the aircraft."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "On October 23, 2019, Ukraine's NBAAI published the final report from South Sudan's Ministry of Transport, concluding that probable causes included bad weather, pilot error in altimeter setting, and an unreported propeller replacement."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "The SSMOT reported that the crew (captain and first officer) lost their logbooks and had not been inspected by South Sudan's Civil Aviation Authority."
    },
    {
      "id": "13",
      "subevent": "The weather forecast for Yirol on the day of the accident indicated fog with visibility less than a few meters, and the crew circled several times before crash landing into Lake Yirol."
    },
    {
      "id": "14",
      "subevent": "The FDR's photosensitive film had ended by the time of the accident, so no flight data was recorded."
    },
    {
      "id": "15",
      "subevent": "The SSMOT released findings including that the aircraft was owned by SlavAir and leased to Southwest, the crew was different from those who ferried the aircraft, propeller maintenance was done without a release certificate, and no weather briefing was obtained."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "Muslims accept that the death penalty is a punishment established by God in the Qur'an, which teaches that capital punishment is acceptable when required by law."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "The Prophet Muhammad sentenced people to death for murder."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Shari'ah law states that the punishment for murder, adultery, and denying Islam is the death penalty."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Some Muslims argue that although capital punishment is allowed in the Qur'an, it is not compulsory."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Shari'ah law allows the family of a murder victim to accept money as reparation rather than enforce the death penalty."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Some Muslims believe that only Allah has the right to take life, and carrying out the death penalty is 'playing God' and committing shirk."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "NASA's Spitzer Space Telescope captured enough light from exoplanets to identify molecules in their atmospheres for the first time, a significant step toward detecting possible life."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "Spitzer project scientist Dr. Michael Werner expressed surprise at the achievement, saying they had no idea Spitzer would make such a dramatic step."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Spitzer obtained spectra for two gas exoplanets, HD 209458b and HD 189733b, and the data indicated they are drier and cloudier than predicted, with no water found."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Astronomers suggested water might be hidden under thick clouds, and HD 209458b showed hints of silicate dust in its atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Dr. Jeremy Richardson, lead author of a Nature paper, stated that water must be hidden by a dusty cloud layer."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Two other teams, led by Dr. Carl Grillmair and Dr. Mark R. Swain, also captured spectra of the exoplanets and obtained similar results."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "A spectrum reveals chemical fingerprints by splitting light into wavelengths; until these observations, only solar system planets had spectra available."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "The exoplanets are extremely far away, with HD 189733b 370 trillion miles and HD 209458b 904 trillion miles from Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "Spitzer used the 'secondary eclipse' technique, measuring the dip in infrared light as planets passed behind their stars, to isolate the planets' light."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "In the studies, Spitzer's spectrograph stared at the transiting planets and subtracted the star's spectrum to obtain the planets' spectra alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Dr. Carl Grillmair noted that the observations were considered high risk but Spitzer performed superbly."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Previous Hubble observations of HD 209458b revealed elements like sodium, with less sodium than predicted, supporting the idea of high clouds."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "Astronomers hope to use Spitzer for further studies of transiting exoplanets, with at least three more candidates for obtaining spectra."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "The vast majority of economists polled by Reuters said President Donald Trump’s import tariffs will do more harm than good to the U.S. economy."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "All 104 economists polled by Reuters expect the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates next week, with three more hikes to follow this year."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Nearly 90 percent of 71 respondents said they were concerned tariffs on steel and aluminum imports would lead to a wider trade war."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Ethan Harris, head of global economics at BofAML, expressed concern about the persistence and scope of protectionism."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Nearly 80 percent of 60 economists said the tariffs would do more harm than good, and none said they would benefit the U.S. economy."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Stefan Koopman of Rabobank warned that the tariffs are likely to be met with retaliation and could undermine global economic expansion."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "The OECD raised its global growth forecasts but warned that the world economy was vulnerable to trade tensions after the import tariffs."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "The U.S. dollar extended its retreat against most major currencies following Trump's tariff decision."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "The Fed is expected to raise the federal funds rate by 25 basis points at the March 20-21 meeting and repeat that move three more times this year."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "The Fed's preferred inflation gauge, the core PCE price index, is forecast to reach 2 percent in the third quarter."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Economic growth is forecast at an annualized rate of 2.5-3.0 percent each quarter this year."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Thirty-one of 58 economists said they were not concerned about fiscal stimulus bringing the next downturn closer, while 27 were concerned."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "The median probability of a recession in the coming year was 13 percent, up from 10 percent in a November poll."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "The ASA ruled that Channel 4's Big Fat Gypsy Weddings poster campaign was irresponsible, offensive, reaffirmed negative stereotypes, and depicted a child in a sexualised way."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "Channel 4's four posters promoted the second series and featured the words 'Bigger. Fatter. Gypsier' over images of Gypsy boys and girls."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Yvonne MacNamara, chief executive of the Irish Traveller Movement in Britain, said that as a result of the decision, Channel 4's brand has been held up to be morally bankrupt and called on the broadcaster to hang its head in shame."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "The Irish Traveller Movement in Britain and co-complainants successfully appealed against the ASA's original decision not to investigate the campaign despite nearly 400 complaints."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "The ASA found that one of the girls in a low-cut top with heavy makeup and visible bra was 15, a year below the legal age for appearing in ads."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "The ASA said Channel 4 acted irresponsibly by depicting a child in a sexualised way."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "The ASA also ruled that the image of an aggressive-looking young boy endorsed negative stereotypes and was likely to cause distress and mental harm to Gypsy and Traveller children."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "Channel 4 said the ad campaign used real and intimate photographic portraits in a journalistic style that reflected the journalistic intent of the series."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "A Channel 4 spokesman apologised for any offense caused and stated that they had taken responsible action pre-publication, including obtaining consent and submitting to the CAP copy advice team."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "Channel 4 chief creative officer Jay Hunt announced that the broadcaster would not make any more series of Big Fat Gypsy Weddings but would produce six Gypsy-themed one-off specials next year."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "In March, media regulator Ofcom dismissed complaints that the TV campaign breached any aspects of the broadcasting code."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "David Enright, a partner at law firm Howe & Co, said the ASA's findings represent a fundamental turning point in how Travellers and Gypsies can be portrayed in the media."
    }
  ]
}

