{
  "subevents": [
    {
      "id": "0",
      "subevent": "Deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon rose more than 50% in the first three months of 2020 compared to the same period in 2019, according to preliminary satellite data."
    },
    {
      "id": "1",
      "subevent": "Scientists warned that Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro's development-friendly policies could transform the Amazon rainforest into a drier, savannah-like landscape once a 'tipping point' is crossed."
    },
    {
      "id": "2",
      "subevent": "Brazil's environmental agency scaled back enforcement measures during the COVID-19 pandemic, leaving the forest and indigenous tribes more vulnerable."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "subevent": "Laercio Guajajara, a member of the Guajajara tribe, told ABC News that their territory is invaded by loggers and hunters, with many invasions ongoing."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "subevent": "Andre Karipuna, a member of the Karipuna tribe, told the Environmental Justice Foundation that the community is desperate because one infected person could spread the virus to the whole tribe."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "subevent": "Bolsonaro slashed funding and enforcement at FUNAI, Brazil's department of indigenous affairs, since taking office in 2019."
    },
    {
      "id": "6",
      "subevent": "In July 2019, Bolsonaro appointed a federal police officer with connections to agribusiness to head FUNAI."
    },
    {
      "id": "7",
      "subevent": "The Guajajara tribe's 'Guardians' conducted patrols to defend their lands, as documented by ABC News in a documentary titled 'Guardians of the Amazon'."
    },
    {
      "id": "8",
      "subevent": "In April 2020, FUNAI passed law IN 09, which prevents indigenous lands awaiting official demarcation from being labelled as 'indigenous' in the land registry."
    },
    {
      "id": "9",
      "subevent": "Environmental groups alleged that law IN 09 provides a route for illegal occupiers of indigenous lands to claim the land is not indigenous."
    },
    {
      "id": "10",
      "subevent": "Bolsonaro's Provisional Measure 910, first decreed in December 2019, allows those who illegally deforested and occupied protected federal lands before December 2018 to purchase the property at reduced prices."
    },
    {
      "id": "11",
      "subevent": "Critics alleged that MP 910 rewards illegal land grabbers, and an analysis by IMAZON found the measure could lead to added deforestation of up to 16,000 square kilometers by 2027."
    },
    {
      "id": "12",
      "subevent": "The Bolsonaro administration denied that MP 910 would facilitate land-grabbing, arguing that new landowners must preserve 80% of their land and only those occupying peacefully for many years would get titles."
    },
    {
      "id": "13",
      "subevent": "Since the enforcement rollbacks, reports of illegal logging and violent attacks across indigenous reserves increased."
    },
    {
      "id": "14",
      "subevent": "In early April, Yanomami reserve leaders reported an increase in illegal gold mining during the pandemic, and BBC Brasil published satellite photos showing miners entering an area with an isolated community."
    },
    {
      "id": "15",
      "subevent": "Some leaders blamed the invasions for the April 10 death of a 15-year-old Yanomami boy, the first known from the tribe to die from the virus."
    },
    {
      "id": "16",
      "subevent": "The Karipuna tribe submitted a complaint to federal prosecutors on April 8 stating that non-indigenous people were clearing inside their reserve."
    },
